# Data-Oriented Design

Design bulk gameplay state around **how it is processed**, not around object taxonomies. CPUs are fast at linear scans over contiguous memory and slow at chasing references.

## Arrays of structs for bulk data

When a system processes many instances per frame (units, projectiles, cards, grid cells), store them as a flat array/list of **structs**, indexed by id — not as a graph of class instances.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — per-instance class objects, pointer-chasing, scattered in memory
public class Projectile
{
    public Transform Owner;
    public float Speed;
    public float Damage;
    public Vector3 Velocity;
}
private List<Projectile> _projectiles;

// ✅ GOOD — contiguous struct storage, processed linearly
public struct ProjectileData
{
    public float3 Position;
    public float3 Velocity;
    public float Damage;
    public int OwnerId;
}
private ProjectileData[] _projectiles;
private int _projectileCount;

private void TickProjectiles(float dt)
{
    for (int i = 0; i < _projectileCount; i++)
    {
        _projectiles[i].Position += _projectiles[i].Velocity * dt;
    }
}
```

## Rules

- **Iterate linearly.** Process one array in one loop; avoid per-element virtual calls, delegates, or dictionary lookups inside the loop body.
- **Separate hot and cold data.** Data touched every frame (position, velocity, timer) lives in one struct; data touched rarely (display name, loadout, config ref) lives elsewhere, joined by index/id. Don't drag cold bytes through the cache.
- **Use ids/indices, not object references,** to relate entities in bulk systems. `int ownerId` beats `Unit owner` in a struct array.
- **Swap-remove** for unordered lists: move last element into the removed slot, decrement count — no `List.RemoveAt(0)` shifting in hot paths.
- MonoBehaviours remain the interface to the scene (rendering, colliders). The *simulation state* they visualize can still live in flat arrays owned by one system that updates all instances in one loop, instead of N `Update()` calls.

## Scope

This applies to systems with many instances processed per frame. A single lobby screen or one player controller does not need SoA gymnastics — keep those simple and direct.

## Agent checklist

- [ ] Bulk per-frame state stored as flat struct arrays/lists, not class graphs
- [ ] Hot loop bodies free of virtual calls, delegate invokes, and dictionary lookups
- [ ] Hot/cold data separated; relations expressed as ids
- [ ] Unordered removal uses swap-remove
